@meta
id: 20-17
title: Paste while typing
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-zero, register-expression
par: 69
minutes: 6
vscode: type half the line, Ctrl-V, type the rest
open: order.provider.ts order.provider.spec.ts

@brief
`p` works in normal mode. In insert mode there is `Ctrl-R` followed by
a register name, which types the register's contents where the cursor
is, as though you had typed them. `Ctrl-R 0` inserts the last yank.
`Ctrl-R a` inserts register `a`. `Ctrl-R =` opens the expression prompt
and inserts the result. Any register works, including `%` and `"`.

Two assertions in the spec are missing their argument:

  1. `toThrow()` in the shipped-order test should carry the code the
     provider throws, as a string: `toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED')`.
     Yank the code from the provider rather than typing it.

  2. `toBe()` in the settle test should be three units at the provider's
     unit price. Let the expression register do the multiplication.

@keys
| `Ctrl-R 0` | insert the last yank, in insert mode |
| `Ctrl-R a` | insert register `a` |
| `Ctrl-R =expr` `Enter` | insert the result of an expression |
| `Ctrl-R "` | insert the unnamed register |

@hints
In the provider, `/ALREADY` Enter and `yiw` takes the whole code —
underscores are word characters. `:bn` to the spec.
--
`/toThrow(` Enter, `f(`, `a` to append inside the parens, then a quote,
`Ctrl-R 0`, a quote, `Esc`. The register types the code for you.
--
`/toBe(` Enter, `f(`, `a`, then `Ctrl-R =3*1849` Enter, `Esc`. `:wqa`.

@solution
    /ALREADY<Enter> yiw  :bn<Enter>
    /toThrow(<Enter> f( a'<Ctrl-R>0'<Esc>
    /toBe(<Enter> f( a<Ctrl-R>=3*1849<Enter><Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Leaving insert mode to put and coming back costs keys and breaks the
line of thought, and for anything more than a word it is also where the
cursor ends up one character off. `Ctrl-R` keeps you typing.

There is a wrinkle. `Ctrl-R` inserts the text as if typed, so
autoindent and any abbreviations apply to it, and a register holding
several lines of code can come out re-indented. `Ctrl-R Ctrl-R 0`
inserts the register literally, and is the one to use for code.

`Ctrl-R` works on the command line too, and that is the next lesson:
a search or a substitute built from what you have already yanked.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

const UNIT_PRICE = 1849;

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async settle(id: string, units: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);
    const total = units * UNIT_PRICE;

    await this.paymentService.capture(order.id, total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SETTLED', total },
    });
  }
}

@start order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { shippedOrder } from './order.fixture';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(shippedOrder);

    await expect(provider.cancel(shippedOrder.id)).rejects.toThrow();
  });

  it('settles three units at the unit price', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(shippedOrder);
    reveal(orderRepository).update.mockImplementation(async ({ values }) => ({ ...shippedOrder, ...values }));

    const result = await provider.settle(shippedOrder.id, 3);

    expect(result.total).toBe();
  });
});

@expected order.provider.spec.ts
import { stub, reveal } from 'jest-auto-stub';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { shippedOrder } from './order.fixture';

describe('OrderProvider', () => {
  const orderRepository = stub<OrderRepository>();
  const paymentService = stub<PaymentService>();
  const provider = new OrderProvider(orderRepository, paymentService);

  it('refuses to cancel a shipped order', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(shippedOrder);

    await expect(provider.cancel(shippedOrder.id)).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
  });

  it('settles three units at the unit price', async () => {
    reveal(orderRepository).findOne.mockResolvedValue(shippedOrder);
    reveal(orderRepository).update.mockImplementation(async ({ values }) => ({ ...shippedOrder, ...values }));

    const result = await provider.settle(shippedOrder.id, 3);

    expect(result.total).toBe(5547);
  });
});
